# Repository line-ending policy.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. This repository was developed on Windows with
# core.autocrlf=true and no .gitattributes. That combination caused a real
# deployment defect twice: `git archive` exported 740 text files with CRLF even
# though the committed blobs are LF, so a "clean checkout of the commit" was not
# byte-identical to the commit. Any tooling that materialises the tree from a
# commit (CI, a release archive, a deploy) inherited the wrong line endings.
#
# WHAT IT DOES - deliberately the minimum.
#
#   text=auto   Git decides text vs binary from content, so binary assets
#               (PNG, JPG, WebP, ICO, the catalogue JSON) are never touched.
#   eol=lf      Text files are checked out and archived with LF, whatever
#               core.autocrlf says on the machine. This is the line ending the
#               blobs already use, so it changes nothing that is committed.
#
# WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO. This commit does NOT run
# `git add --renormalize .` and does NOT re-save a single existing file. Adding
# the policy changes no blob and, because every blob is already LF, marks
# nothing as modified. Anyone who wants a bulk renormalisation should do it as
# its own reviewed change; folding hundreds of file rewrites into a release
# commit would make the diff unreviewable.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary assets: never end-of-line converted, never diffed as text.
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.webp   binary
*.gif    binary
*.ico    binary
*.pdf    binary
*.woff   binary
*.woff2  binary
*.ttf    binary
*.otf    binary
*.mp4    binary
*.webm   binary

# Lockfiles are generated and must match the registry byte for byte.
package-lock.json -text

# The catalogue exports and the MEKO/PDF/OCR artefacts are large generated
# inputs. They are stored as-is and must never be line-ending converted.
catalogue-source/** binary
data/source-catalogue/** binary
data/meko-*/** binary
data/menon-brakes-catalogue/** binary
data/pensol-catalogue/** binary
CATALOGUE binary
